d  it's synthesized by our nih  collaborators the group of terry burke  in collaboration with the group of steve  hughes  4d is a leading preclinical candidate  and in fact it has higher potency than  big tegravir against many drug resistant  variants  so with 4d we can see that the core  scaffold is shifted closer to the  magnesium ions  and that's because it's based on  distinct chemistry where  electronegative atoms are actually part  of the central nectarine core  we also see this long extension on the  left side of the molecule  this extension is very important it  contributes  quite significantly to viral potency it  also constitutes the region  that we are actively exploring and  modifying  in order to try and identify compounds  with higher potency  against viral resistant variants  now i already mentioned water molecules  we we see them throughout the active  site  this slide shows the density and the  location  for some of the bound water molecules in  the active site of the april form  uh the april form contains calcium as  well  as well as three of the nc bound forms  these institute form  bound forms contain magnesium in the  active site  medicinal chemists like to look at  figures like this which simplify the  analysis  this is a two dimensional schematic of  how the enzyme binds to the into some  active site  instant interactions and waters  and waters can both be subdivided by  their relative position with respect to  the  plane that is formed by the magnesium  coordinating the  light the ligand scaffold so there's a  core naftaridine scaffold this chelates  the magnesium ions  and also contains the two r groups r2  contains the halogenated benzoylmoidy  stacks against viral dna  the r1 group that's the one that we're  very interested in  and the group that we're actively  modifying  to elicit better binding and better  response  it points toward the bulk solvent  what's important is that the positioning  of its long chain  displaces loosely bound waters now i'm  not showing the data here  but in addition to the structural  interpretations we actually have some md  data that confirms  the water molecules in the bottom left  region of the schematic are loosely  bound  and displacement of these loosely bound  solvent  solvent molecules should be entropically  advantageous  for the compounds  there's a large network of water  molecules above the core scaffold  and also below the core scaffold and we  believe that  both of these networks can potentially  be exploited for the purposes of drug  design  so this is where future computational  chemistry efforts will be important in  order to really understand the  energetics of binding  and the favorability of water  displacement and we're actively  investigating  this area in collaboration with stefano  fourly  at the scripps research institute  so i do want to note that while the hiv  structures have certainly been very  informative  we did know quite a bit about  insta-binding through earlier work  about a decade ago the chiropractor  group solved structures of prototype  foamy virus antisomes  these are endosomes from a simpler  retrovirus  and the complexes were also assembled  and solved with several bound institutes  we learned a lot from that early work a  lot about  instant binding and and also about  mechanisms of viral resistance  the problem is that the active sites of  hiv  and prototype foamy virus synthesis are  not conserved  depending on how you align the  conservation of the catalytic core  domain is around 20 or 30  and there's and there's only about a 40  conservation  in the residues that are mutated by the  virus when  it involves resistance against  dynasty-based therapy  this is really important it's important  because when you overlay the two active  sites they're not identical  this figure shows the rmsd between the  prototype formula virus into some active  site  and the hiv to some active site and it's  mapped onto the structure of the ladder