nformation  for drug design  or for understanding mechanisms of viral  resistance  we have to be working with the correct  construct  this is really critical as i mentioned  before the residues in the active site  are not necessarily conserved  between prototyphomeovirus and hiv  endosomes and this is indicated in the  table  only about 40 of the residues are  identical  those are indicated in green and many of  these residues  are not cons that are not conserved also  happen to be  viral resistant variants and we know  that drug resistance is a major problem  this is a table showing just a small  subset of patient-derived  viral resistant variants from instant  usage in the clinic  there are many emergent drug-resistant  variants  and more that we are likely to see in  the future  here's just a look at some of the  structural differences between the  resistant variants  each one of these panels shows an  overlay of drug resistant variants in  hiv integrase and the corresponding  residues of pfe and grace  you can see that the conformations are  not identical  the differences might be minor if you  think in terms of atomic displacement  however as i showed just a few slides  ago their effects can really be profound  so they can completely alter how the  compound binds in the active site  so if we want to understand the atomic  mechanism  by which resistant variants displace the  compound again we  really need to be working with the real  construct  where are we going with this work many  of you probably know  that we can measure small molecular  efficacy in cells  using ec50 analysis in the last several  slides  i kept emphasizing the point about  resistance  so if we look at how the naftering  compounds perform  to inhibit hiv challenge in cellular  assays  particularly when the virus contains  clinically relevant resistant variants  what one sees is that several of the  naftavidian compounds especially  compound 4d  outperform the clinically used drugs  this is important because all of the  compounds  potently inhibit the wild-type virus  even the poor compounds potentially  inhibit wild type virus  however what we're interested in is not  necessarily inhibiting the wild-type  virus  we've been doing that extremely well for  the last decade  we're interested in how the compounds  inhibit mutant virus that has already  evolved resistance  that's what's going to be relevant for  long-lasting therapy for patients  so we hope that the naftarian compounds  may provide  an avenue towards this goal  in the future what we want to do is we  want to understand how resistant  variants affect  its dividing we want to decipher the  atomic mechanisms that are involved in  this process  we want to use the criaem pipeline for  the purposes of drug design  so we believe that with structural  biology  computational chemistry medicinal  chemistry  we can potentially make a dent in this  area  i just want to finish up with this last  slide  this slide describes the strategy that  we are planning to take to achieve this  goal  it's based on an idea called the  substrate envelope hypothesis  this is an idea that was developed by  celia schiffer's lab  and it was applied to the development of  protease inhibitors  about 15 or 20 years ago the substrate  envelope  is the molecular envelope of an enzyme  that binds  natural substrates so any and all  natural substrates  in the case of protease those substrates  are the amino acids that are  proteolytically cleaved  by the enzyme in the case of integrase  natural substrates are both viral  and target dna and these and these are  displayed in the current figure  so a resistant variant needs to displace  the bond drug  when a resistant mutation arises if it  displaces the drug  while at the same time protruding into  the natural substrate envelope  this can potentially compromise viral  fitness  therefore the resistant variant has less  of a chance to persist in the clonal  viral population  so if you 